"""
The inline spans of the Markdown text of one block, with their source
ranges, and the reveal rule of a live editor: a span shows its Markdown
source while the cursor is in it and is drawn rendered otherwise.

The syntax follows CommonMark 0.31.2, sections 6.1 to 6.7, and GFM (GitHub
Flavored Markdown) for strikethrough and extended autolinks.  Code spans,
autolinks (an absolute URI, Uniform Resource Identifier, or an e-mail
address in angle brackets) and raw HTML (HyperText Markup Language) tags
bind first, from
left to right; then links and images; then emphasis, strong emphasis and
strikethrough by the rules of delimiter runs (left and right flanking, the
rule of multiples of 3, no intraword emphasis with _).  A span may cross a
line break: the text may hold LF (line feed) bytes.

The text is UTF-8 (Unicode Transformation Format, 8 bit).  Every offset is
a byte offset into it; every span starts and stops at an ASCII (American
Standard Code for Information Interchange) byte or at the end, so no span
splits a UTF-8 sequence.  A span covers start to stop, stop being the
byte after it, markers included; its inner part is what a reader sees when
it is rendered (see innerStart).  Spans come in the order of their start,
an enclosing span before the spans inside it; spanParent names the
enclosing one.  Spans nest properly: two spans are disjoint or one holds
the other.

A reference link counts only when its label is among the labels passed to
scanSpans: one label per line, normalised the way this module normalises
the label in the text (ASCII and Latin-1 capitals as small letters, every
run of white space one blank, none at the ends).

Limits:
- At most mostSpanBytes bytes are read; the rest of the text has no spans.
- At most mostSpans spans are kept; later ones are dropped.
- Entity names are checked for their form (a letter, then 1 to 30 letters
  and digits), not against the list of HTML entity names.
- Label folding knows ASCII and Latin-1 capitals only, not the full Unicode
  case folding.
- A footnote reference [^id] is a span whatever the labels say; its label
  holds no blank, no bracket and at most 999 bytes.
- An emoji :name: is a span for any name of a-z, 0-9, _, + and -; the name
  is not checked against a list.
- Bare links (GFM extended autolinks) know the lowercase prefixes www.,
  http:// and https:// and ASCII domains only, and no bare e-mail address;
  as in the GFM reference parser, none is found while a [ or ![ is open.
- Strikethrough takes runs of one or two ~; an opener and its closer have
  the same length.
- A URL (Uniform Resource Locator) is not checked for safety here: an
  unsafe link is still a link span.

    let spans: Spans := scanSpans("a **b** c", "");
    let first: Index := spanStart(&spans, 0);
    let shown: Bool := isRevealed(&spans, 0, 4);
    destroySpans(spans);
"""
module Wolly.MarkdownSpans is
    """
    The kind of a span.  EmphasisSpan, StrongSpan and StrikeSpan come from
    delimiter runs; CodeSpan from backticks; LinkSpan and ImageSpan from
    brackets; AutolinkSpan from an absolute URI or an e-mail address in
    angle brackets; BareLinkSpan from a www., http:// or https:// link
    without brackets; HtmlSpan from an inline raw HTML tag, comment,
    processing instruction, declaration or CDATA (character data) section;
    EscapeSpan from a backslash before ASCII punctuation; EntitySpan from
    an entity or a numeric character reference; FootnoteSpan from [^id];
    EmojiSpan from :name:.
    """
    union SpanKind: Free is
        case EmphasisSpan;
        case StrongSpan;
        case StrikeSpan;
        case CodeSpan;
        case LinkSpan;
        case ImageSpan;
        case AutolinkSpan;
        case BareLinkSpan;
        case HtmlSpan;
        case EscapeSpan;
        case EntitySpan;
        case FootnoteSpan;
        case EmojiSpan;
    end;

    """
    The spans of one text, in the order of their start, outer before inner.
    Linear: destroySpans gives them back.
    """
    type Spans: Linear;

    """
    The most spans scanSpans keeps: 65536.
    """
    function mostSpans(): Index
        ensures result = 65536;

    """
    The most bytes of the text scanSpans reads: 1048576.
    """
    function mostSpanBytes(): Index
        ensures result = 1048576;

    """
    The spans of text by the rules of the module head; labels holds the
    known reference labels, one per line, normalised; the empty span means
    none.
    """
    generic [R: Region, L: Region]
    function scanSpans(text: Span[Nat8, R], labels: Span[Nat8, L]): Spans
        ensures spanCount(&result) <= mostSpans();

    """
    Gives the spans back.
    No contract: it only frees what it is given.
    """
    function destroySpans(spans: Spans): Unit;

    """
    How many spans there are.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function spanCount(spans: &[Spans, R]): Index
        ensures result <= mostSpans();

    """
    The kind of span i.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function spanKind(spans: &[Spans, R], i: Index): SpanKind
        requires i < spanCount(spans);

    """
    The first byte of span i, its opening marker.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function spanStart(spans: &[Spans, R], i: Index): Index
        requires i < spanCount(spans)
        ensures result < mostSpanBytes();

    """
    The byte after span i, after its closing marker.  Always greater than
    spanStart of the span.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function spanStop(spans: &[Spans, R], i: Index): Index
        requires i < spanCount(spans)
        ensures (result >= 1) and (result <= mostSpanBytes());

    """
    The first byte of the part of span i shown when it is rendered: for
    emphasis, strong, strikethrough and a code span the text between the
    markers (for a code span without the one blank stripped at either end);
    for a link and an image the text inside the brackets; for an escape the
    escaped byte; for an entity, an autolink, a bare link, an HTML tag, a
    footnote and an emoji the whole span.  Never before spanStart.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function innerStart(spans: &[Spans, R], i: Index): Index
        requires i < spanCount(spans)
        ensures result <= mostSpanBytes();

    """
    The byte after the part of span i shown when it is rendered.  Never
    before innerStart and never after spanStop.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function innerStop(spans: &[Spans, R], i: Index): Index
        requires i < spanCount(spans)
        ensures result <= mostSpanBytes();

    """
    The index of the span that encloses span i most closely, or spanCount
    when none does.  An enclosing span comes before the spans inside it.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function spanParent(spans: &[Spans, R], i: Index): Index
        requires i < spanCount(spans)
        ensures (result < i) or (result = spanCount(spans));

    """
    Whether span i shows its source for a cursor at the byte offset cursor:
    when spanStart <= cursor <= spanStop, both ends included, so a cursor
    right after the closing marker still reveals it.  Every span that
    encloses a revealed span is revealed too, since it holds the cursor.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function isRevealed(spans: &[Spans, R], i: Index, cursor: Index): Bool
        requires i < spanCount(spans);

    """
    Whether span i shows its source for the selection from selection_start
    to selection_stop: when the span, ends included, overlaps the selection
    or holds one of its ends.  For an empty selection the same as
    isRevealed.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function isRevealedBy(spans: &[Spans, R], i: Index, selection_start: Index, selection_stop: Index): Bool
        requires (i < spanCount(spans)) and (selection_start <= selection_stop);
end module.
